{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016vGVfKricmR1YTKkuyRno9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UKsqcPxRsKo66aASy4AjBj","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Cardinality-Constrained KV Cache Quantization: Preventing 2-Bit Compounding Failures on Hardware-Aligned Bit-Widths\"\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. **2-bit compounding is universal**: Independent per-head profiling underestimates joint distortion when multiple heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit. Per-head profiling predicts joint PPL≈11 but actual joint 2-bit PPL is 32–37 (3× prediction error). All unconstrained solvers consequently over-allocate 2-bit heads and underperform uniform 4-bit across 8/8 tested architectures.\n2. **A cardinality constraint is the universal fix—and makes solver choice irrelevant**: Capping the number of 2-bit heads at k ≤ 8 prevents compounding across all architectures. Under this constraint, greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations (Δ=0.0000 PPL), eliminating solver complexity.\n3. **Non-uniform benefit is rare**: Under moderate (4×) compression, only 1/8 architectures—those with a \"spread\" sensitivity pattern—benefits from non-uniform allocation. A two-metric diagnostic correctly predicts this for all 9 tested models, providing practitioners a simple check before investing in profiling.\n\n## Method Details (§3)\n\n**Problem Formulation (§3.1)**: Given H KV heads, bit-width options B = {2,4,8,16}, minimize total distortion subject to memory budget. Standard MCKP formulation. Independent per-head profiling assumes distortions are additive: D({b_h}) = Σ_h d_h(b_h). Paper shows this assumption fails when multiple heads are assigned 2-bit simultaneously.\n\n**2-Bit Compounding Effect (§3.2)**: When k heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit, D_joint(k) >> Σ d_h(2). Characterized empirically via compounding sweep: varying k from 0 to H. On Qwen2.5-7B, profiling predicts joint PPL≈11 at k=112 but actual PPL is 98.75 (9× prediction error).\n\n**Cardinality-Constrained Allocation (§3.3)**: Augment MCKP with |{h : b_h = 2}| ≤ k. Determine k by sweeping on calibration set, select largest k with <2.5% degradation. Empirically, k ≤ 8 is universally safe.\n\n**Sensitivity Distribution Diagnostic (§3.4)**: Two-metric diagnostic:\n- Top-5% concentration: fraction of total sensitivity in top 5% of heads. Threshold ≥ 0.50.\n- Dominance ratio t₁/t₅: ratio of single most sensitive head to sum of top-5 heads. Threshold ≤ 0.70.\n- Joint condition identifies \"spread\" patterns where multiple heads have high but differentiated sensitivity.\n\n## Experiments (§4)\n\n**Setup (§4.1)**: 8 architectures spanning GQA ratios 1:1 to 8:1: Qwen2.5-7B, Yi-1.5-9B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-1.4B, Qwen2.5-14B, Phi-3-medium. WikiText-2 at 4× compression. High-resolution profiling: 64 sequences × 2048 tokens.\n\n**Table 1 (§4.2)**: Main results. All unconstrained solvers underperform uniform 4-bit across all architectures. Degradation is concentration-dependent: catastrophic on Qwen (3.31×), moderate on Gemma-2 (1.47×), mild on Yi/Llama/Mistral (1.07–1.08×).\n\n**Table 2 (§4.3)**: Solver equivalence. On Qwen-7B, greedy unconstrained PPL=32.45, MCKP unconstrained PPL=36.92. With k=8, both produce identical allocations {2:8, 4:100, 8:4} and identical PPL (11.2312). Replicates on Qwen-14B (Δ=0.0000).\n\n**Table 3 (§4.4)**: Compounding sweep on Qwen. U-shaped: k=8 is sweet spot (2.4% degradation), k=64 causes 2.34×, k=112 causes 10.07×.\n\n**Table 4**: Cross-architecture compounding at k=8. All <2.5% degradation. Qwen U-shaped, Llama monotonic, Mistral flat-monotonic.\n\n**Table 5**: Top-k vs random-k compounding. Both exhibit super-linear growth. random-k worse due to correlated heads within same layer.\n\n**Table 6 (§4.5)**: Sensitivity distribution diagnostic. 9/9 accuracy. Only Qwen meets both conditions (conc≥0.50, t₁/t₅≤0.70).\n\n**Table 7 (§4.6)**: Budget sweep. 2×: +3.4%, 4×: +29.4%, 8×: >97% improvement.\n\n**Table 8 (§4.7)**: Long-context (8K). Uniform degrades 11.15×, MCKP only 1.23×. 9× gap.\n\n**Table 9 (§4.8)**: Downstream eval on Qwen (ARC-C, HellaSwag, PIQA, WinoGrande). +1.25 to +4.00pp gains. 72.8% average recovery.\n\n**Table 10**: Cross-architecture downstream. 5/5 diagnostic consistency. Non-spread show minor degradation.\n\n**Table 11**: Heuristic baselines. MCKP outperforms L0-random and naive positional on all 3 models. But MCKP beats uniform only on Qwen.\n\n**Table 12 (§4.10)**: Runtime. 8.5% decode latency overhead for mixed-precision vs uniform 4-bit. Same memory (5.25 MB).\n\n**Table 13 (§4.11)**: KIVI comparison. KIVI 4-bit (g32, r128) PPL=9.90 vs constrained MCKP 10.91. Approaches are complementary (per-channel vs per-head).\n\n**Ablation Studies (§4.9)**:\n- L0 exclusion removal: solver auto-discovers L0 sensitivity, PPL identical (10.91)\n- Profiling resolution: 64×2048 yields PPL 10.91 vs 16×512 PPL 11.23 (2.9% improvement)\n- K/V separation: separate K/V yields PPL 11.22 vs joint 10.91\n- Oracle comparison: max gap 0.018 PPL (0.18% of baseline), MCKP exactly optimal on 5/8 layer groups\n- Cross-dataset calibration: C4 vs WikiText-2, 96.4% allocation agreement (108/112 heads)\n- Multi-seed CIs: PPL 10.99±0.065, identical macro allocation\n- Mistral k ablation: monotonically increasing with k\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\n\nThe review_context.yaml lists these imagined experiments. Check which are covered in the paper:\n1. L0 exclusion removal — Covered in §4.9\n2. Llama-3.1-8B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n3. Mistral-7B-v0.3 full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n4. Compounding sweep — Covered in Table 3\n5. Budget sweep — Covered in Table 7\n6. High-resolution profiling — Covered in §4.9\n7. C4 cross-dataset — Covered in §4.9\n8. RateQuant greedy comparison — Covered in Table 2 (greedy unconstrained)\n9. Oracle comparison — Covered in §4.9\n10. Cross-domain calibration — Merged with #7\n11. Head sensitivity visualization — Not explicitly as a figure (no figures in the PDF text, but referenced)\n12. Downstream task eval (Qwen) — Covered in Table 9\n13. Qwen2.5-72B profiling — Mentioned as resource-limited in Limitations\n14. K/V separation — Covered in §4.9\n15. L0-only heuristic baseline — Covered in Table 11\n16. Greedy + constraint ablation — Covered in Table 2\n17. Multi-seed error bars — Covered in §4.8\n18. Runtime/latency benchmark — Covered in Table 12\n19. Gemma-2-9B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n20. Yi-1.5-9B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n21. Cross-architecture compounding sweeps — Covered in Table 4\n22. 8K long-context evaluation — Covered in Table 8\n23. Qwen-14B pipeline + solver equivalence — Covered in Table 1 and Table 2\n24. Pythia-1.4B pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n25. Phi-3-medium profiling — Covered in Table 6\n26. CodeLlama profiling — Covered in Table 6\n27. KIVI baseline — Covered in Table 13\n28. Cross-architecture downstream — Covered in Table 10\n29. Qwen 3-seed downstream CI — Covered in §4.8\n30. W9 compounding scaling — Covered in Table 5\n\n## Your Review\n\nProvide your review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered list, focus on method soundness and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered list, focus on method limitations and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n5. **Minor Issues** (numbered list)\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate whether the method is sound, experiments are sufficient, and claims are adequately supported. Consider: statistical rigor, baselines, ablations, generalizability, reproducibility.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32974,"cache_read_input_tokens":24124,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32974},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7b9a637-3199-45af-a202-1f5fee8b9b34","uuid":"a824bd0a-9d4c-47b5-aed7-991c52e7f848"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a5af2e0476cd3f846","tool_use_id":"toolu_01UKsqcPxRsKo66aASy4AjBj","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Cardinality-Constrained KV Cache Quantization: Preventing 2-Bit Compounding Failures on Hardware-Aligned Bit-Widths\"\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. **2-bit compounding is universal**: Independent per-head profiling underestimates joint distortion when multiple heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit. Per-head profiling predicts joint PPL≈11 but actual joint 2-bit PPL is 32–37 (3× prediction error). All unconstrained solvers consequently over-allocate 2-bit heads and underperform uniform 4-bit across 8/8 tested architectures.\n2. **A cardinality constraint is the universal fix—and makes solver choice irrelevant**: Capping the number of 2-bit heads at k ≤ 8 prevents compounding across all architectures. Under this constraint, greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations (Δ=0.0000 PPL), eliminating solver complexity.\n3. **Non-uniform benefit is rare**: Under moderate (4×) compression, only 1/8 architectures—those with a \"spread\" sensitivity pattern—benefits from non-uniform allocation. A two-metric diagnostic correctly predicts this for all 9 tested models, providing practitioners a simple check before investing in profiling.\n\n## Method Details (§3)\n\n**Problem Formulation (§3.1)**: Given H KV heads, bit-width options B = {2,4,8,16}, minimize total distortion subject to memory budget. Standard MCKP formulation. Independent per-head profiling assumes distortions are additive: D({b_h}) = Σ_h d_h(b_h). Paper shows this assumption fails when multiple heads are assigned 2-bit simultaneously.\n\n**2-Bit Compounding Effect (§3.2)**: When k heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit, D_joint(k) >> Σ d_h(2). Characterized empirically via compounding sweep: varying k from 0 to H. On Qwen2.5-7B, profiling predicts joint PPL≈11 at k=112 but actual PPL is 98.75 (9× prediction error).\n\n**Cardinality-Constrained Allocation (§3.3)**: Augment MCKP with |{h : b_h = 2}| ≤ k. Determine k by sweeping on calibration set, select largest k with <2.5% degradation. Empirically, k ≤ 8 is universally safe.\n\n**Sensitivity Distribution Diagnostic (§3.4)**: Two-metric diagnostic:\n- Top-5% concentration: fraction of total sensitivity in top 5% of heads. Threshold ≥ 0.50.\n- Dominance ratio t₁/t₅: ratio of single most sensitive head to sum of top-5 heads. Threshold ≤ 0.70.\n- Joint condition identifies \"spread\" patterns where multiple heads have high but differentiated sensitivity.\n\n## Experiments (§4)\n\n**Setup (§4.1)**: 8 architectures spanning GQA ratios 1:1 to 8:1: Qwen2.5-7B, Yi-1.5-9B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-1.4B, Qwen2.5-14B, Phi-3-medium. WikiText-2 at 4× compression. High-resolution profiling: 64 sequences × 2048 tokens.\n\n**Table 1 (§4.2)**: Main results. All unconstrained solvers underperform uniform 4-bit across all architectures. Degradation is concentration-dependent: catastrophic on Qwen (3.31×), moderate on Gemma-2 (1.47×), mild on Yi/Llama/Mistral (1.07–1.08×).\n\n**Table 2 (§4.3)**: Solver equivalence. On Qwen-7B, greedy unconstrained PPL=32.45, MCKP unconstrained PPL=36.92. With k=8, both produce identical allocations {2:8, 4:100, 8:4} and identical PPL (11.2312). Replicates on Qwen-14B (Δ=0.0000).\n\n**Table 3 (§4.4)**: Compounding sweep on Qwen. U-shaped: k=8 is sweet spot (2.4% degradation), k=64 causes 2.34×, k=112 causes 10.07×.\n\n**Table 4**: Cross-architecture compounding at k=8. All <2.5% degradation. Qwen U-shaped, Llama monotonic, Mistral flat-monotonic.\n\n**Table 5**: Top-k vs random-k compounding. Both exhibit super-linear growth. random-k worse due to correlated heads within same layer.\n\n**Table 6 (§4.5)**: Sensitivity distribution diagnostic. 9/9 accuracy. Only Qwen meets both conditions (conc≥0.50, t₁/t₅≤0.70).\n\n**Table 7 (§4.6)**: Budget sweep. 2×: +3.4%, 4×: +29.4%, 8×: >97% improvement.\n\n**Table 8 (§4.7)**: Long-context (8K). Uniform degrades 11.15×, MCKP only 1.23×. 9× gap.\n\n**Table 9 (§4.8)**: Downstream eval on Qwen (ARC-C, HellaSwag, PIQA, WinoGrande). +1.25 to +4.00pp gains. 72.8% average recovery.\n\n**Table 10**: Cross-architecture downstream. 5/5 diagnostic consistency. Non-spread show minor degradation.\n\n**Table 11**: Heuristic baselines. MCKP outperforms L0-random and naive positional on all 3 models. But MCKP beats uniform only on Qwen.\n\n**Table 12 (§4.10)**: Runtime. 8.5% decode latency overhead for mixed-precision vs uniform 4-bit. Same memory (5.25 MB).\n\n**Table 13 (§4.11)**: KIVI comparison. KIVI 4-bit (g32, r128) PPL=9.90 vs constrained MCKP 10.91. Approaches are complementary (per-channel vs per-head).\n\n**Ablation Studies (§4.9)**:\n- L0 exclusion removal: solver auto-discovers L0 sensitivity, PPL identical (10.91)\n- Profiling resolution: 64×2048 yields PPL 10.91 vs 16×512 PPL 11.23 (2.9% improvement)\n- K/V separation: separate K/V yields PPL 11.22 vs joint 10.91\n- Oracle comparison: max gap 0.018 PPL (0.18% of baseline), MCKP exactly optimal on 5/8 layer groups\n- Cross-dataset calibration: C4 vs WikiText-2, 96.4% allocation agreement (108/112 heads)\n- Multi-seed CIs: PPL 10.99±0.065, identical macro allocation\n- Mistral k ablation: monotonically increasing with k\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\n\nThe review_context.yaml lists these imagined experiments. Check which are covered in the paper:\n1. L0 exclusion removal — Covered in §4.9\n2. Llama-3.1-8B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n3. Mistral-7B-v0.3 full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n4. Compounding sweep — Covered in Table 3\n5. Budget sweep — Covered in Table 7\n6. High-resolution profiling — Covered in §4.9\n7. C4 cross-dataset — Covered in §4.9\n8. RateQuant greedy comparison — Covered in Table 2 (greedy unconstrained)\n9. Oracle comparison — Covered in §4.9\n10. Cross-domain calibration — Merged with #7\n11. Head sensitivity visualization — Not explicitly as a figure (no figures in the PDF text, but referenced)\n12. Downstream task eval (Qwen) — Covered in Table 9\n13. Qwen2.5-72B profiling — Mentioned as resource-limited in Limitations\n14. K/V separation — Covered in §4.9\n15. L0-only heuristic baseline — Covered in Table 11\n16. Greedy + constraint ablation — Covered in Table 2\n17. Multi-seed error bars — Covered in §4.8\n18. Runtime/latency benchmark — Covered in Table 12\n19. Gemma-2-9B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n20. Yi-1.5-9B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n21. Cross-architecture compounding sweeps — Covered in Table 4\n22. 8K long-context evaluation — Covered in Table 8\n23. Qwen-14B pipeline + solver equivalence — Covered in Table 1 and Table 2\n24. Pythia-1.4B pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n25. Phi-3-medium profiling — Covered in Table 6\n26. CodeLlama profiling — Covered in Table 6\n27. KIVI baseline — Covered in Table 13\n28. Cross-architecture downstream — Covered in Table 10\n29. Qwen 3-seed downstream CI — Covered in §4.8\n30. W9 compounding scaling — Covered in Table 5\n\n## Your Review\n\nProvide your review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered list, focus on method soundness and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered list, focus on method limitations and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n5. **Minor Issues** (numbered list)\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate whether the method is sound, experiments are sufficient, and claims are adequately supported. Consider: statistical rigor, baselines, ablations, generalizability, reproducibility.","uuid":"176cc1d9-d4b7-4518-ae4d-c5baef0c0602","session_id":"b7b9a637-3199-45af-a202-1f5fee8b9b34"}
