{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UKsqcPxRsKo66aASy4AjBj","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have read all sections of the paper. Let me compile my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Cardinality-Constrained KV Cache Quantization\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper identifies a \"2-bit compounding\" failure mode in per-head KV cache quantization with hardware-aligned bit-widths {2,4,8,16}: when unconstrained solvers assign too many heads to 2-bit, distortions compound super-additively through the residual stream, causing catastrophic PPL degradation. The proposed fix is a cardinality constraint capping 2-bit assignments at k heads. The paper further provides a two-metric diagnostic (top-5% concentration and t1/t5 ratio) to predict whether non-uniform allocation benefits a given architecture, finding that only 1 of 9 tested models (Qwen2.5-7B) exhibits the \"spread\" pattern where gains are meaningful.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n1. **Well-identified failure mode.** The 2-bit compounding phenomenon is clearly demonstrated and convincingly established. The gap between additive prediction (~11 PPL) and actual joint 2-bit PPL (32-37) on Qwen is stark and reproducible. The paper does not just identify the problem but provides a mechanistic explanation (super-additive error accumulation through the residual stream).\n\n2. **Admirably honest framing.** The paper is unusually forthcoming about the limited practical impact of its method: 8/9 models show no benefit from non-uniform allocation. The N=1 spread limitation is flagged in the abstract, contributions, analysis, and limitations. This level of intellectual honesty is rare and commendable.\n\n3. **Thorough ablation suite.** The paper covers an impressive range of ablations: L0 exclusion, profiling resolution (standard vs. hires), K/V separation, oracle comparison, cross-dataset stability (WikiText-2 vs. C4), multi-seed confidence intervals, budget sweep, long-context generalization, runtime benchmarks, heuristic baselines, and cross-architecture compounding sweeps. Each addresses a natural question a reader would have.\n\n4. **Solver equivalence result.** The finding that constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations is a useful practical insight. The verification across three architectures (112/112, 256/256, 255/256 head agreement) is convincing, and the per-layer brute-force oracle (max gap 0.018 PPL) closes the optimality chain cleanly.\n\n5. **Reproducibility.** The experimental setup is described in sufficient detail (model configurations, profiling parameters, calibration set sizes, evaluation protocols). Multi-seed results (3 seeds, PPL 10.99+/-0.06) and cross-dataset stability (96-97% allocation agreement) support reproducibility.\n\n6. **Cross-architecture breadth.** Seven models across five families, spanning MHA (1:1) to GQA (8:1), with diverse attention mechanisms (standard, sliding-window, softcapped). The within-family scale contrast (Qwen-7B vs. 14B) is particularly informative.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n1. **N=1 positive result fundamentally limits the contribution.** The entire non-uniform allocation benefit rests on a single model (Qwen2.5-7B). While the paper is honest about this, it means the diagnostic rule has exactly one positive training example and zero held-out positives. The 9/9 classification accuracy is inflated by the 8 easy negatives. No reviewer can evaluate whether the concentration >= 0.50 and t1/t5 <= 0.70 thresholds generalize, because there is no second spread model. This is the paper's central scientific limitation.\n\n2. **No theoretical grounding for compounding.** The paper characterizes compounding purely empirically (sweeping k, measuring PPL). There is no formal model of why errors compound super-additively through the residual stream, no analysis of when the additive assumption breaks down as a function of per-head distortion magnitude, and no connection to existing theory on error propagation in transformer architectures. The paper states that \"truncation errors accumulate through the residual stream\" but does not formalize this. A simple analytical model (even approximate) would significantly strengthen the contribution.\n\n3. **Comparison with KIVI undermines practical motivation.** KIVI 4-bit achieves 9.90 PPL on Qwen vs. the proposed method's 10.91, and KIVI+MCKP achieves the same 9.90 as KIVI alone. The paper acknowledges this and argues composability/orthogonality, but the practical implication is clear: if a practitioner has access to asymmetric per-channel quantization (which is widely supported), the per-head allocation provides zero additional benefit. The paper positions per-head allocation as valuable in \"symmetric-only deployment environments,\" but does not quantify how common such environments are or provide examples of real hardware that lacks per-channel asymmetric support.\n\n4. **Quantization scheme is simplistic.** The paper uses symmetric per-channel quantization without per-token scaling, which is known to be suboptimal for KV cache quantization. This inflates the baseline PPL (Qwen uniform 4-bit: 15.45 vs. KIVI 4-bit: 9.90) and may artificially amplify the compounding effect. It is unclear whether 2-bit compounding occurs at all under more sophisticated quantization schemes (e.g., asymmetric per-channel with per-token residual). The paper does not test this.\n\n5. **Long-context evaluation is limited.** 8K tokens is far below production deployment lengths (32K-128K). The dramatic Qwen result at 8K (uniform 11.15x vs. MCKP 1.23x) is compelling but raises the question of whether this is driven by the simplistic quantization scheme rather than the allocation method. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but it weakens the generalizability claims.\n\n6. **Downstream evaluation scope.** Only four 0-shot classification benchmarks, no generation tasks. For KV cache quantization, generation quality (e.g., summarization, translation, chat) is arguably more relevant than classification accuracy, since KV cache errors accumulate during autoregressive generation. ARC-C results show the largest sensitivity but with no statistical significance testing across seeds for non-Qwen models.\n\n7. **Missing comparison with concurrent/recent work.** The related work section lists KVmix, KVTuner, PMKVQ, Kitty, and others operating at different granularities, but no empirical comparison is made with any of them. Even a comparison at layer-level granularity (KVmix) would help position the contribution. The paper argues these are \"orthogonal\" but that claim deserves empirical support.\n\n8. **Runtime overhead is measured in simulation only.** The 8.5% decode overhead uses PyTorch-level dispatch, not actual mixed-precision kernels. The paper acknowledges this is an upper bound, but without real-kernel measurements, practitioners cannot assess deployment feasibility. This is especially concerning given that the KIVI comparison already shows per-head allocation provides zero additional benefit when advanced quantization is available.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you tested whether 2-bit compounding occurs under asymmetric per-channel quantization (e.g., KIVI-style)? If the compounding is an artifact of the symmetric quantization scheme, the practical relevance of the cardinality constraint diminishes significantly.\n\n2. Can you provide any theoretical argument (even heuristic) for why the compounding onset occurs around k=8-32 rather than, say, k=1 or k=64? Is there a relationship between compounding onset and model width, depth, or attention configuration?\n\n3. The diagnostic thresholds (concentration >= 0.50, t1/t5 <= 0.70) are calibrated on 9 models with 1 positive. What happens if both thresholds are relaxed by 10%? Does the classification still hold? How sensitive is the diagnostic to threshold choice?\n\n4. For the Qwen-7B/14B contrast, you attribute the concentration dilution to 3.4x more heads. Have you tested intermediate scales (e.g., Qwen-3B if available) to establish whether this is a continuous relationship or a phase transition?\n\n5. Table 2 footnote states unconstrained rows use standard-resolution profiling while constrained rows use high-resolution. This asymmetry in profiling resolution makes the comparison unfair. What are the unconstrained results with high-resolution profiling?\n\n6. The paper claims \"per-head allocation provides independent value in symmetric-only deployment environments.\" Can you name specific hardware accelerators or inference engines that support only symmetric quantization? How large is this deployment niche?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n1. Table 1 caption states \"8 architectures\" but lists 7 models (Phi-3-medium is in Table 6 but not Table 1). The abstract says \"nine models\" (including CodeLlama and Phi-3-medium for profiling-only). The counting is inconsistent across sections.\n\n2. The Qwen MCKP PPL in Table 2 (heuristic baselines, Appendix) is 11.23 while Table 1 reports 10.91. This reflects the standard vs. hires profiling difference, but it is confusing that different tables use different profiling resolutions without always flagging which.\n\n3. Table 5 (top-k vs. random-k) claims \"random-k worse due to correlated heads within same layer,\" but the actual result shows random-k is worse (55.94 vs. 22.95 at k=64), which the text attributes to \"random draws are more likely to include high-distortion heads.\" These two explanations seem to contradict each other. Please clarify.\n\n4. The compounding figure (Figure 2, Appendix) is referenced in the main text (Section 3.3) but is only in the appendix. Given that compounding is the paper's central phenomenon, this figure should be in the main body.\n\n5. \"BOS-corrected\" for Gemma-2 is mentioned in tables but never fully explained in the main text.\n\n6. Some experimental details are hard to trace: the k values per architecture (k=8 for Qwen/Gemma/Pythia, k=2 for Yi/Llama/Mistral) appear in Section 4.1 but the sweeps justifying these choices are only partially shown (Table 3 for Qwen, Table 4 summary for others, Mistral k-ablation in appendix).\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThe paper makes a clear, well-supported empirical observation (2-bit compounding) and proposes a simple, effective fix (cardinality constraint). The experimental coverage is exceptionally thorough for a single-method paper, and the intellectual honesty about the N=1 limitation is commendable.\n\nHowever, the contribution is fundamentally constrained by three factors: (a) the positive result applies to only 1 of 9 models, making the paper primarily a negative result about the limited utility of per-head mixed-precision KV cache quantization; (b) the KIVI comparison demonstrates that per-head allocation adds nothing when modern quantization schemes are available, narrowing the practical niche to symmetric-only deployments that the paper does not characterize; and (c) the lack of theoretical grounding for the compounding phenomenon limits the paper's explanatory power beyond the empirical observations.\n\nThe paper is well-executed and would be of interest to the KV cache quantization community as a cautionary tale and diagnostic tool. The experimental methodology sets a good standard for this subfield. But for a top venue at 25% acceptance, the limited scope of the positive result and the narrow practical applicability present challenges. The paper reads more as a thorough empirical study of a specific failure mode than as a broadly impactful methodological advance.\n\nThe honest framing ultimately works in the paper's favor: the claims are precise and well-calibrated to the evidence. If the authors had overclaimed, this would be a clear reject; the careful scoping makes it borderline.\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I am familiar with KV cache quantization methods, mixed-precision allocation, and the MCKP formulation. I have read the paper in its entirety including all appendices. I am less certain about the practical hardware landscape for symmetric-only deployment, which affects my assessment of practical relevance."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5af2e0476cd3f846 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5af2e0476cd3f846' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 44279\ntool_uses: 9\nduration_ms: 98271</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7b9a637-3199-45af-a202-1f5fee8b9b34","uuid":"ee322239-fd7a-4ad0-9f2a-4cd3a80ef429","timestamp":"2026-05-16T02:48:21.304Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Cardinality-Constrained KV Cache Quantization: Preventing 2-Bit Compounding Failures on Hardware-Aligned Bit-Widths\"\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. **2-bit compounding is universal**: Independent per-head profiling underestimates joint distortion when multiple heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit. Per-head profiling predicts joint PPL≈11 but actual joint 2-bit PPL is 32–37 (3× prediction error). All unconstrained solvers consequently over-allocate 2-bit heads and underperform uniform 4-bit across 8/8 tested architectures.\n2. **A cardinality constraint is the universal fix—and makes solver choice irrelevant**: Capping the number of 2-bit heads at k ≤ 8 prevents compounding across all architectures. Under this constraint, greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations (Δ=0.0000 PPL), eliminating solver complexity.\n3. **Non-uniform benefit is rare**: Under moderate (4×) compression, only 1/8 architectures—those with a \"spread\" sensitivity pattern—benefits from non-uniform allocation. A two-metric diagnostic correctly predicts this for all 9 tested models, providing practitioners a simple check before investing in profiling.\n\n## Method Details (§3)\n\n**Problem Formulation (§3.1)**: Given H KV heads, bit-width options B = {2,4,8,16}, minimize total distortion subject to memory budget. Standard MCKP formulation. Independent per-head profiling assumes distortions are additive: D({b_h}) = Σ_h d_h(b_h). Paper shows this assumption fails when multiple heads are assigned 2-bit simultaneously.\n\n**2-Bit Compounding Effect (§3.2)**: When k heads are simultaneously quantized to 2-bit, D_joint(k) >> Σ d_h(2). Characterized empirically via compounding sweep: varying k from 0 to H. On Qwen2.5-7B, profiling predicts joint PPL≈11 at k=112 but actual PPL is 98.75 (9× prediction error).\n\n**Cardinality-Constrained Allocation (§3.3)**: Augment MCKP with |{h : b_h = 2}| ≤ k. Determine k by sweeping on calibration set, select largest k with <2.5% degradation. Empirically, k ≤ 8 is universally safe.\n\n**Sensitivity Distribution Diagnostic (§3.4)**: Two-metric diagnostic:\n- Top-5% concentration: fraction of total sensitivity in top 5% of heads. Threshold ≥ 0.50.\n- Dominance ratio t₁/t₅: ratio of single most sensitive head to sum of top-5 heads. Threshold ≤ 0.70.\n- Joint condition identifies \"spread\" patterns where multiple heads have high but differentiated sensitivity.\n\n## Experiments (§4)\n\n**Setup (§4.1)**: 8 architectures spanning GQA ratios 1:1 to 8:1: Qwen2.5-7B, Yi-1.5-9B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-1.4B, Qwen2.5-14B, Phi-3-medium. WikiText-2 at 4× compression. High-resolution profiling: 64 sequences × 2048 tokens.\n\n**Table 1 (§4.2)**: Main results. All unconstrained solvers underperform uniform 4-bit across all architectures. Degradation is concentration-dependent: catastrophic on Qwen (3.31×), moderate on Gemma-2 (1.47×), mild on Yi/Llama/Mistral (1.07–1.08×).\n\n**Table 2 (§4.3)**: Solver equivalence. On Qwen-7B, greedy unconstrained PPL=32.45, MCKP unconstrained PPL=36.92. With k=8, both produce identical allocations {2:8, 4:100, 8:4} and identical PPL (11.2312). Replicates on Qwen-14B (Δ=0.0000).\n\n**Table 3 (§4.4)**: Compounding sweep on Qwen. U-shaped: k=8 is sweet spot (2.4% degradation), k=64 causes 2.34×, k=112 causes 10.07×.\n\n**Table 4**: Cross-architecture compounding at k=8. All <2.5% degradation. Qwen U-shaped, Llama monotonic, Mistral flat-monotonic.\n\n**Table 5**: Top-k vs random-k compounding. Both exhibit super-linear growth. random-k worse due to correlated heads within same layer.\n\n**Table 6 (§4.5)**: Sensitivity distribution diagnostic. 9/9 accuracy. Only Qwen meets both conditions (conc≥0.50, t₁/t₅≤0.70).\n\n**Table 7 (§4.6)**: Budget sweep. 2×: +3.4%, 4×: +29.4%, 8×: >97% improvement.\n\n**Table 8 (§4.7)**: Long-context (8K). Uniform degrades 11.15×, MCKP only 1.23×. 9× gap.\n\n**Table 9 (§4.8)**: Downstream eval on Qwen (ARC-C, HellaSwag, PIQA, WinoGrande). +1.25 to +4.00pp gains. 72.8% average recovery.\n\n**Table 10**: Cross-architecture downstream. 5/5 diagnostic consistency. Non-spread show minor degradation.\n\n**Table 11**: Heuristic baselines. MCKP outperforms L0-random and naive positional on all 3 models. But MCKP beats uniform only on Qwen.\n\n**Table 12 (§4.10)**: Runtime. 8.5% decode latency overhead for mixed-precision vs uniform 4-bit. Same memory (5.25 MB).\n\n**Table 13 (§4.11)**: KIVI comparison. KIVI 4-bit (g32, r128) PPL=9.90 vs constrained MCKP 10.91. Approaches are complementary (per-channel vs per-head).\n\n**Ablation Studies (§4.9)**:\n- L0 exclusion removal: solver auto-discovers L0 sensitivity, PPL identical (10.91)\n- Profiling resolution: 64×2048 yields PPL 10.91 vs 16×512 PPL 11.23 (2.9% improvement)\n- K/V separation: separate K/V yields PPL 11.22 vs joint 10.91\n- Oracle comparison: max gap 0.018 PPL (0.18% of baseline), MCKP exactly optimal on 5/8 layer groups\n- Cross-dataset calibration: C4 vs WikiText-2, 96.4% allocation agreement (108/112 heads)\n- Multi-seed CIs: PPL 10.99±0.065, identical macro allocation\n- Mistral k ablation: monotonically increasing with k\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\n\nThe review_context.yaml lists these imagined experiments. Check which are covered in the paper:\n1. L0 exclusion removal — Covered in §4.9\n2. Llama-3.1-8B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n3. Mistral-7B-v0.3 full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n4. Compounding sweep — Covered in Table 3\n5. Budget sweep — Covered in Table 7\n6. High-resolution profiling — Covered in §4.9\n7. C4 cross-dataset — Covered in §4.9\n8. RateQuant greedy comparison — Covered in Table 2 (greedy unconstrained)\n9. Oracle comparison — Covered in §4.9\n10. Cross-domain calibration — Merged with #7\n11. Head sensitivity visualization — Not explicitly as a figure (no figures in the PDF text, but referenced)\n12. Downstream task eval (Qwen) — Covered in Table 9\n13. Qwen2.5-72B profiling — Mentioned as resource-limited in Limitations\n14. K/V separation — Covered in §4.9\n15. L0-only heuristic baseline — Covered in Table 11\n16. Greedy + constraint ablation — Covered in Table 2\n17. Multi-seed error bars — Covered in §4.8\n18. Runtime/latency benchmark — Covered in Table 12\n19. Gemma-2-9B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n20. Yi-1.5-9B full pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n21. Cross-architecture compounding sweeps — Covered in Table 4\n22. 8K long-context evaluation — Covered in Table 8\n23. Qwen-14B pipeline + solver equivalence — Covered in Table 1 and Table 2\n24. Pythia-1.4B pipeline — Covered in Table 1\n25. Phi-3-medium profiling — Covered in Table 6\n26. CodeLlama profiling — Covered in Table 6\n27. KIVI baseline — Covered in Table 13\n28. Cross-architecture downstream — Covered in Table 10\n29. Qwen 3-seed downstream CI — Covered in §4.8\n30. W9 compounding scaling — Covered in Table 5\n\n## Your Review\n\nProvide your review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered list, focus on method soundness and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered list, focus on method limitations and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n5. **Minor Issues** (numbered list)\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate whether the method is sound, experiments are sufficient, and claims are adequately supported. Consider: statistical rigor, baselines, ablations, generalizability, reproducibility.","agentId":"a5af2e0476cd3f846","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have read all sections of the paper. Let me compile my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Cardinality-Constrained KV Cache Quantization\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper identifies a \"2-bit compounding\" failure mode in per-head KV cache quantization with hardware-aligned bit-widths {2,4,8,16}: when unconstrained solvers assign too many heads to 2-bit, distortions compound super-additively through the residual stream, causing catastrophic PPL degradation. The proposed fix is a cardinality constraint capping 2-bit assignments at k heads. The paper further provides a two-metric diagnostic (top-5% concentration and t1/t5 ratio) to predict whether non-uniform allocation benefits a given architecture, finding that only 1 of 9 tested models (Qwen2.5-7B) exhibits the \"spread\" pattern where gains are meaningful.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n1. **Well-identified failure mode.** The 2-bit compounding phenomenon is clearly demonstrated and convincingly established. The gap between additive prediction (~11 PPL) and actual joint 2-bit PPL (32-37) on Qwen is stark and reproducible. The paper does not just identify the problem but provides a mechanistic explanation (super-additive error accumulation through the residual stream).\n\n2. **Admirably honest framing.** The paper is unusually forthcoming about the limited practical impact of its method: 8/9 models show no benefit from non-uniform allocation. The N=1 spread limitation is flagged in the abstract, contributions, analysis, and limitations. This level of intellectual honesty is rare and commendable.\n\n3. **Thorough ablation suite.** The paper covers an impressive range of ablations: L0 exclusion, profiling resolution (standard vs. hires), K/V separation, oracle comparison, cross-dataset stability (WikiText-2 vs. C4), multi-seed confidence intervals, budget sweep, long-context generalization, runtime benchmarks, heuristic baselines, and cross-architecture compounding sweeps. Each addresses a natural question a reader would have.\n\n4. **Solver equivalence result.** The finding that constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations is a useful practical insight. The verification across three architectures (112/112, 256/256, 255/256 head agreement) is convincing, and the per-layer brute-force oracle (max gap 0.018 PPL) closes the optimality chain cleanly.\n\n5. **Reproducibility.** The experimental setup is described in sufficient detail (model configurations, profiling parameters, calibration set sizes, evaluation protocols). Multi-seed results (3 seeds, PPL 10.99+/-0.06) and cross-dataset stability (96-97% allocation agreement) support reproducibility.\n\n6. **Cross-architecture breadth.** Seven models across five families, spanning MHA (1:1) to GQA (8:1), with diverse attention mechanisms (standard, sliding-window, softcapped). The within-family scale contrast (Qwen-7B vs. 14B) is particularly informative.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n1. **N=1 positive result fundamentally limits the contribution.** The entire non-uniform allocation benefit rests on a single model (Qwen2.5-7B). While the paper is honest about this, it means the diagnostic rule has exactly one positive training example and zero held-out positives. The 9/9 classification accuracy is inflated by the 8 easy negatives. No reviewer can evaluate whether the concentration >= 0.50 and t1/t5 <= 0.70 thresholds generalize, because there is no second spread model. This is the paper's central scientific limitation.\n\n2. **No theoretical grounding for compounding.** The paper characterizes compounding purely empirically (sweeping k, measuring PPL). There is no formal model of why errors compound super-additively through the residual stream, no analysis of when the additive assumption breaks down as a function of per-head distortion magnitude, and no connection to existing theory on error propagation in transformer architectures. The paper states that \"truncation errors accumulate through the residual stream\" but does not formalize this. A simple analytical model (even approximate) would significantly strengthen the contribution.\n\n3. **Comparison with KIVI undermines practical motivation.** KIVI 4-bit achieves 9.90 PPL on Qwen vs. the proposed method's 10.91, and KIVI+MCKP achieves the same 9.90 as KIVI alone. The paper acknowledges this and argues composability/orthogonality, but the practical implication is clear: if a practitioner has access to asymmetric per-channel quantization (which is widely supported), the per-head allocation provides zero additional benefit. The paper positions per-head allocation as valuable in \"symmetric-only deployment environments,\" but does not quantify how common such environments are or provide examples of real hardware that lacks per-channel asymmetric support.\n\n4. **Quantization scheme is simplistic.** The paper uses symmetric per-channel quantization without per-token scaling, which is known to be suboptimal for KV cache quantization. This inflates the baseline PPL (Qwen uniform 4-bit: 15.45 vs. KIVI 4-bit: 9.90) and may artificially amplify the compounding effect. It is unclear whether 2-bit compounding occurs at all under more sophisticated quantization schemes (e.g., asymmetric per-channel with per-token residual). The paper does not test this.\n\n5. **Long-context evaluation is limited.** 8K tokens is far below production deployment lengths (32K-128K). The dramatic Qwen result at 8K (uniform 11.15x vs. MCKP 1.23x) is compelling but raises the question of whether this is driven by the simplistic quantization scheme rather than the allocation method. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but it weakens the generalizability claims.\n\n6. **Downstream evaluation scope.** Only four 0-shot classification benchmarks, no generation tasks. For KV cache quantization, generation quality (e.g., summarization, translation, chat) is arguably more relevant than classification accuracy, since KV cache errors accumulate during autoregressive generation. ARC-C results show the largest sensitivity but with no statistical significance testing across seeds for non-Qwen models.\n\n7. **Missing comparison with concurrent/recent work.** The related work section lists KVmix, KVTuner, PMKVQ, Kitty, and others operating at different granularities, but no empirical comparison is made with any of them. Even a comparison at layer-level granularity (KVmix) would help position the contribution. The paper argues these are \"orthogonal\" but that claim deserves empirical support.\n\n8. **Runtime overhead is measured in simulation only.** The 8.5% decode overhead uses PyTorch-level dispatch, not actual mixed-precision kernels. The paper acknowledges this is an upper bound, but without real-kernel measurements, practitioners cannot assess deployment feasibility. This is especially concerning given that the KIVI comparison already shows per-head allocation provides zero additional benefit when advanced quantization is available.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you tested whether 2-bit compounding occurs under asymmetric per-channel quantization (e.g., KIVI-style)? If the compounding is an artifact of the symmetric quantization scheme, the practical relevance of the cardinality constraint diminishes significantly.\n\n2. Can you provide any theoretical argument (even heuristic) for why the compounding onset occurs around k=8-32 rather than, say, k=1 or k=64? Is there a relationship between compounding onset and model width, depth, or attention configuration?\n\n3. The diagnostic thresholds (concentration >= 0.50, t1/t5 <= 0.70) are calibrated on 9 models with 1 positive. What happens if both thresholds are relaxed by 10%? Does the classification still hold? How sensitive is the diagnostic to threshold choice?\n\n4. For the Qwen-7B/14B contrast, you attribute the concentration dilution to 3.4x more heads. Have you tested intermediate scales (e.g., Qwen-3B if available) to establish whether this is a continuous relationship or a phase transition?\n\n5. Table 2 footnote states unconstrained rows use standard-resolution profiling while constrained rows use high-resolution. This asymmetry in profiling resolution makes the comparison unfair. What are the unconstrained results with high-resolution profiling?\n\n6. The paper claims \"per-head allocation provides independent value in symmetric-only deployment environments.\" Can you name specific hardware accelerators or inference engines that support only symmetric quantization? How large is this deployment niche?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n1. Table 1 caption states \"8 architectures\" but lists 7 models (Phi-3-medium is in Table 6 but not Table 1). The abstract says \"nine models\" (including CodeLlama and Phi-3-medium for profiling-only). The counting is inconsistent across sections.\n\n2. The Qwen MCKP PPL in Table 2 (heuristic baselines, Appendix) is 11.23 while Table 1 reports 10.91. This reflects the standard vs. hires profiling difference, but it is confusing that different tables use different profiling resolutions without always flagging which.\n\n3. Table 5 (top-k vs. random-k) claims \"random-k worse due to correlated heads within same layer,\" but the actual result shows random-k is worse (55.94 vs. 22.95 at k=64), which the text attributes to \"random draws are more likely to include high-distortion heads.\" These two explanations seem to contradict each other. Please clarify.\n\n4. The compounding figure (Figure 2, Appendix) is referenced in the main text (Section 3.3) but is only in the appendix. Given that compounding is the paper's central phenomenon, this figure should be in the main body.\n\n5. \"BOS-corrected\" for Gemma-2 is mentioned in tables but never fully explained in the main text.\n\n6. Some experimental details are hard to trace: the k values per architecture (k=8 for Qwen/Gemma/Pythia, k=2 for Yi/Llama/Mistral) appear in Section 4.1 but the sweeps justifying these choices are only partially shown (Table 3 for Qwen, Table 4 summary for others, Mistral k-ablation in appendix).\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThe paper makes a clear, well-supported empirical observation (2-bit compounding) and proposes a simple, effective fix (cardinality constraint). The experimental coverage is exceptionally thorough for a single-method paper, and the intellectual honesty about the N=1 limitation is commendable.\n\nHowever, the contribution is fundamentally constrained by three factors: (a) the positive result applies to only 1 of 9 models, making the paper primarily a negative result about the limited utility of per-head mixed-precision KV cache quantization; (b) the KIVI comparison demonstrates that per-head allocation adds nothing when modern quantization schemes are available, narrowing the practical niche to symmetric-only deployments that the paper does not characterize; and (c) the lack of theoretical grounding for the compounding phenomenon limits the paper's explanatory power beyond the empirical observations.\n\nThe paper is well-executed and would be of interest to the KV cache quantization community as a cautionary tale and diagnostic tool. The experimental methodology sets a good standard for this subfield. But for a top venue at 25% acceptance, the limited scope of the positive result and the narrow practical applicability present challenges. The paper reads more as a thorough empirical study of a specific failure mode than as a broadly impactful methodological advance.\n\nThe honest framing ultimately works in the paper's favor: the claims are precise and well-calibrated to the evidence. If the authors had overclaimed, this would be a clear reject; the careful scoping makes it borderline.\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I am familiar with KV cache quantization methods, mixed-precision allocation, and the MCKP formulation. I have read the paper in its entirety including all appendices. I am less certain about the practical hardware landscape for symmetric-only deployment, which affects my assessment of practical relevance."}],"totalDurationMs":98271,"totalTokens":44279,"totalToolUseCount":9,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1668,"cache_read_input_tokens":39691,"output_tokens":2919,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1668},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2919,"cache_read_input_tokens":39691,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1668,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1668,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
