{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SFE1pB4pH9UP7SBVYE8sk3","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/related_work.tex:4:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:10:Complementary to quantization, eviction methods reduce cache size by discarding tokens---retaining heavy-hitters~\\cite{zhang2023h2o}, per-head important tokens~\\cite{li2024snapkv,ge2024fastgen}, persistent-importance tokens~\\cite{liu2024scissorhands}, attention sinks~\\cite{xiao2024streamingllm}, or progressively fewer tokens in deeper layers~\\cite{yang2024pyramidinfer}.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:11:Recent per-head eviction methods allocate non-uniform budgets across heads based on retrieval-vs-reasoning importance~\\cite{fu2025headkv} or adaptive optimization extending SnapKV/PyramidKV~\\cite{feng2025adakv}, confirming that head-level heterogeneity is exploitable for eviction; our work shows it is equally exploitable for quantization.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:16:HAWQ~\\cite{dong2019hawq} and HAWQ-V2~\\cite{dong2020hawqv2} use second-order (Hessian-based) sensitivity to assign per-layer bit-widths, with V2 introducing block-wise trace-weighted scoring and Pareto-frontier selection; HAQ~\\cite{wang2019haq} learns an allocation by reinforcement learning under hardware-aware latency/energy constraints.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:22:RateQuant~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant} formulates per-head bit-width selection as an MCKP and proves greedy optimality for consecutive bit-widths $\\{2,\\ldots,6\\}$ via the polymatroid structure, which relies on the assumption that per-head distortions decompose additively into the global objective---valid when adjacent bit-widths differ by a single bit so that quantization noise stays in the small-perturbation regime.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:26:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:29:Prior work studies head redundancy and pruning~\\cite{voita2019analyzing,michel2019sixteen}, focusing on which heads can be removed entirely.\ndocs/paper/experiments.tex:33:(2)~unconstrained greedy allocation (RateQuant-style~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant});\ndocs/paper/experiments.tex:82:KIVI~\\cite{liu2024kivi} uses asymmetric per-channel quantization with per-token scaling, applied uniformly across all heads.\ndocs/paper/method.tex:17:RateQuant~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant} solves this for consecutive bit-widths $\\{2,3,4,5,6\\}$, where the polymatroid structure of the feasible set guarantees that greedy allocation is optimal (their Proposition 6).\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:61:\\emph{Layer-level} allocation (KVmix~\\cite{li2025kvmix}, KVTuner~\\cite{li2025kvtuner}) keeps the search space small ($L$ layers) but forces all heads in a layer to share one bitwidth.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:64:\\emph{Token-level} allocation (ZipCache~\\cite{he2024zipcache}, KIVI~\\cite{liu2024kivi}) is the finest grain available but introduces runtime overhead from online importance scoring.\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:5:Recent work on KV cache quantization explores both uniform schemes~\\cite{liu2024kivi,hooper2024kvquant,lin2024qserve,he2024zipcache} and token-level mixed-precision approaches~\\cite{ge2024fastgen,lei2026arkv}, while complementary methods reduce cache size through eviction~\\cite{zhang2023h2o,li2024snapkv}.\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:6:RateQuant~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant} formalizes per-head allocation as a rate-distortion optimization and shows that greedy allocation is optimal when bit-widths are consecutive integers $\\{2,3,4,5,6\\}$, exploiting the polymatroid structure of the feasible set (their Proposition~6).\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:42:[Omitted long matching line]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7b9a637-3199-45af-a202-1f5fee8b9b34","uuid":"5cb2966d-1761-4775-b775-8edb02ec20f2","timestamp":"2026-05-16T02:50:06.082Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"docs/paper/related_work.tex:4:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:10:Complementary to quantization, eviction methods reduce cache size by discarding tokens---retaining heavy-hitters~\\cite{zhang2023h2o}, per-head important tokens~\\cite{li2024snapkv,ge2024fastgen}, persistent-importance tokens~\\cite{liu2024scissorhands}, attention sinks~\\cite{xiao2024streamingllm}, or progressively fewer tokens in deeper layers~\\cite{yang2024pyramidinfer}.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:11:Recent per-head eviction methods allocate non-uniform budgets across heads based on retrieval-vs-reasoning importance~\\cite{fu2025headkv} or adaptive optimization extending SnapKV/PyramidKV~\\cite{feng2025adakv}, confirming that head-level heterogeneity is exploitable for eviction; our work shows it is equally exploitable for quantization.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:16:HAWQ~\\cite{dong2019hawq} and HAWQ-V2~\\cite{dong2020hawqv2} use second-order (Hessian-based) sensitivity to assign per-layer bit-widths, with V2 introducing block-wise trace-weighted scoring and Pareto-frontier selection; HAQ~\\cite{wang2019haq} learns an allocation by reinforcement learning under hardware-aware latency/energy constraints.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:22:RateQuant~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant} formulates per-head bit-width selection as an MCKP and proves greedy optimality for consecutive bit-widths $\\{2,\\ldots,6\\}$ via the polymatroid structure, which relies on the assumption that per-head distortions decompose additively into the global objective---valid when adjacent bit-widths differ by a single bit so that quantization noise stays in the small-perturbation regime.\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:26:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/related_work.tex:29:Prior work studies head redundancy and pruning~\\cite{voita2019analyzing,michel2019sixteen}, focusing on which heads can be removed entirely.\ndocs/paper/experiments.tex:33:(2)~unconstrained greedy allocation (RateQuant-style~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant});\ndocs/paper/experiments.tex:82:KIVI~\\cite{liu2024kivi} uses asymmetric per-channel quantization with per-token scaling, applied uniformly across all heads.\ndocs/paper/method.tex:17:RateQuant~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant} solves this for consecutive bit-widths $\\{2,3,4,5,6\\}$, where the polymatroid structure of the feasible set guarantees that greedy allocation is optimal (their Proposition 6).\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:61:\\emph{Layer-level} allocation (KVmix~\\cite{li2025kvmix}, KVTuner~\\cite{li2025kvtuner}) keeps the search space small ($L$ layers) but forces all heads in a layer to share one bitwidth.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:64:\\emph{Token-level} allocation (ZipCache~\\cite{he2024zipcache}, KIVI~\\cite{liu2024kivi}) is the finest grain available but introduces runtime overhead from online importance scoring.\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:5:Recent work on KV cache quantization explores both uniform schemes~\\cite{liu2024kivi,hooper2024kvquant,lin2024qserve,he2024zipcache} and token-level mixed-precision approaches~\\cite{ge2024fastgen,lei2026arkv}, while complementary methods reduce cache size through eviction~\\cite{zhang2023h2o,li2024snapkv}.\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:6:RateQuant~\\cite{zuo2026ratequant} formalizes per-head allocation as a rate-distortion optimization and shows that greedy allocation is optimal when bit-widths are consecutive integers $\\{2,3,4,5,6\\}$, exploiting the polymatroid structure of the feasible set (their Proposition~6).\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:42:[Omitted long matching line]","numLines":15}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DfPrbodsaDSBc9Zv1SqGBa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the actual LaTeX source files have many more citations than what appeared in the PDF. The PDF seems to have been generated from a different/earlier version or the rendered version I saw was truncated. The actual paper has:\n\nIn introduction.tex: liu2024kivi, hooper2024kvquant, lin2024qserve, he2024zipcache, ge2024fastgen, lei2026arkv, zhang2023h2o, li2024snapkv, zuo2026ratequant\nIn related_work.tex: zhang2023h2o, li2024snapkv, ge2024fastgen, liu2024scissorhands, xiao2024streamingllm, yang2024pyramidinfer, fu2025headkv, feng2025adakv, dong2019hawq, dong2020hawqv2, wang2019haq, zuo2026ratequant, and many more (kim2024squeezellm, duanmu2024skvq, su2025rotat\n\n, ekv, etc.)\nIn appendix.tex: li2025kvmix, li2025kvtuner, he2024zipcache, liu2024kivi\nIn experiments.tex: zuo2026ratequant, liu2024kivi\n\nSo R2's concern about \"2 citations\" is incorrect — the paper actually cites 31 references. The PDF rendering I saw apparently showed a condensed version. R1 was correct that the bibliography is adequate.\n\nThis is an important correction for the roundtable. Now I'm moving on to synthesize the final report and identify the key convergence points between reviewers, starting with their agreement on the compounding failure mode analysis.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on several critical issues: the compounding failure mode is well-characterized, the N=1 positive result is the main limitation, the diagnostic needs more validation, and the lack of theoretical grounding is a significant gap. They diverge on whether the negative result itself constitutes a sufficient contribution. With R1 scoring 4 (Weak Accept) and R2 scoring 3 (Weak Reject), I'm settling on a meta-score of 3.5 — the paper has genuine empirical value but limited methodological novelty — and I'll write up the full report with this synthesis.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2147,"cache_read_input_tokens":77180,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2147},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7b9a637-3199-45af-a202-1f5fee8b9b34","uuid":"a13fca80-e21d-4313-8c19-37d57788c0cf"}
